Practical Mining Scenario: Preventing a Haul-Truck Collision
Consider an illustrative open-pit mining operation with heavy haul trucks, light vehicles and maintenance personnel moving between work areas.

Over several weeks, the safety team receives multiple observations about poor visibility at a busy intersection.

A conventional reporting process might treat these as separate findings.

In a connected digital EHS environment, the observations can be grouped, reviewed alongside inspection findings and escalated for risk assessment.

The resulting actions may include traffic-flow changes, improved road signage, additional physical separation, revised operating procedures and targeted worker briefings.

Management can then track completion and verify whether the controls are effective.

AI-supported analytics may help identify the recurring pattern sooner, but the preventive measures still depend on competent assessment, engineering decisions and operational accountability.

This is the practical meaning of proactive mining safety: using available information to strengthen controls before a serious event occurs.

